slang: --relax-forward-method-refs

"identifier 'f' used before its declaration" (UsedBeforeDeclared) for a class method that
returns a value, named without parentheses (`x = get_op;`) before its declaration. slang
lets a task, a void function or a call with parentheses be used before its declaration,
but not this form, which other tools accept. The new CompilationFlags::RelaxForwardMethodRefs
(driver option --relax-forward-method-refs) makes Lookup::unqualifiedImpl accept a forward
reference to a subroutine or method prototype found in a class scope. Found in OpenTitan's
keymgr and kmac DV. Applies on top of 0012 (same enum).

--- a/include/slang/ast/Compilation.h
+++ b/include/slang/ast/Compilation.h
@@ -161,9 +161,13 @@ enum class SLANG_EXPORT CompilationFlags {
 
     /// Allow a string to be compared with an integral operand (e.g. a byte of another
     /// string); the integral operand is converted to a string, as by a cast.
-    RelaxStringCompare = 1 << 24
+    RelaxStringCompare = 1 << 24,
+
+    /// Allow a class method that returns a value to be named, without parentheses,
+    /// before its declaration.
+    RelaxForwardMethodRefs = 1 << 25
 };
-SLANG_BITMASK(CompilationFlags, RelaxStringCompare)
+SLANG_BITMASK(CompilationFlags, RelaxForwardMethodRefs)
 
 /// Contains various options that can control compilation behavior.
